sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-pa02-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Animals, with scarcely an exception and conspicuously all those capable of locomotion, have a stomach-sac that serves them as an internal substitute for the earth from which plants draw food.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Nearly all animals, and all that move about, have a stomach";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, On the Parts of Animals (De Partibus Animalium), Book II, Part 3; translated by William Ogle, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. V (1912). Scope: all animals, especially those that move from place to place"
}
